1
00:00:10,391 --> 00:00:11,092
Good evening.

2
00:00:11,433 --> 00:00:13,215
I hope you had a fruitful day.

3
00:00:13,236 --> 00:00:17,001
I do not say happy, but I hope it has been a happy day.

4
00:00:18,184 --> 00:00:33,868
Namo Tassa Bhagavato Harahato Sambha Sambhodasa Namo Tassa Bhagavato Harahato Sambha Sambhodasa

5
00:00:34,642 --> 00:00:57,442
homage to the buddha the blessed noble and fully self-awakened one so uh these past few days we've been uh looking at anatta as a um

6
00:00:57,625 --> 00:01:25,658
an intellectual exercise really just trying to understand it trying to understand the way the buddha expresses it in the scriptures and uh hopefully that's become uh more clear become more clear to me trying to teach it frankly but now we have to get down to the practice so how how do we actually investigate this in terms of our uh vipassana about practice so uh many of you who who've worked with me of course will

7
00:01:25,856 --> 00:01:30,429
will have heard me speak about it and explain it.

8
00:01:31,251 --> 00:01:34,399
So for you, it's a bit of a revision, you might say.

9
00:01:34,859 --> 00:01:42,498
But for those who haven't, it might offer you a technique, a way of investigating not-self.

10
00:01:47,305 --> 00:01:51,509
Uh, so there's three things, first of all, deconstruction, right?

11
00:01:51,528 --> 00:01:53,349
To deconstruct what we experienced.

12
00:01:53,990 --> 00:02:01,456
The second one is, um, uh, control seeing, seeing that what control we have, what control we don't have.

13
00:02:02,197 --> 00:02:13,326
Uh, remember that was one of the, of the, and that's a lack of, so to the, that second discourse on not self, he starts off by saying, if this body were me, I'd be able to say, don't fall ill, et cetera, et cetera.

14
00:02:13,866 --> 00:02:16,889
And the final, and the third one is objectification.

15
00:02:17,527 --> 00:02:19,710
to turn everything into an object.

16
00:02:19,770 --> 00:02:21,411
If it's an object, it can't be me.

17
00:02:22,431 --> 00:02:29,337
So when we talk about deconstruction, we can take a very simple object.

18
00:02:29,657 --> 00:02:39,447
Everybody suffers from pain in the knee, usually, unless you're a yoga person.

19
00:02:40,306 --> 00:02:44,110
It took me years to be able to sit without any pain in my knees.

20
00:02:45,632 --> 00:02:47,112
So, okay, the pain comes.

21
00:02:47,566 --> 00:02:49,368
It draws your attention.

22
00:02:50,069 --> 00:02:55,217
And the first thing is, of course, your reaction, which is not to want the pain, the desire to move.

23
00:02:56,319 --> 00:02:58,543
So you have to wait for that to die away.

24
00:02:58,963 --> 00:03:00,004
Just recognize it.

25
00:03:00,126 --> 00:03:01,467
Desiring, desiring.

26
00:03:03,009 --> 00:03:03,770
Feel it, of course.

27
00:03:03,830 --> 00:03:05,413
Feel the desire to move.

28
00:03:05,574 --> 00:03:06,895
It's all about feeling.

29
00:03:06,915 --> 00:03:15,109
And then when that's passed and you find yourself equanimous, that's the time to start using the word pain.

30
00:03:16,539 --> 00:03:20,424
Okay, so we're using the word pain or discomfort, whatever.

31
00:03:20,443 --> 00:03:23,747
And of course, we're not trying to get rid of pain or defeat pain, right?

32
00:03:23,787 --> 00:03:25,989
That's a no-no.

33
00:03:26,008 --> 00:03:28,270
And if the pain gets too much, well, you move, right?

34
00:03:28,290 --> 00:03:30,413
Make the intention to move and off you go.

35
00:03:31,033 --> 00:03:32,814
But let's say that the pain is bearable.

36
00:03:33,496 --> 00:03:35,698
So you've got this pain, pain.

37
00:03:36,237 --> 00:03:42,183
And then what you have to do is deconstruct it, by which we mean you go into the pain.

38
00:03:42,824 --> 00:03:45,467
You try and see what it's actually made of.

39
00:03:45,768 --> 00:03:48,030
And when you get in there, you might find heat.

40
00:03:48,670 --> 00:03:50,133
You might find tightness.

41
00:03:50,152 --> 00:03:51,574
You might find pressure.

42
00:03:52,555 --> 00:03:56,699
You might find little bits of sparkly sensations.

43
00:03:56,919 --> 00:03:59,562
All sorts of little things might happen.

44
00:04:00,122 --> 00:04:01,764
And your noting word might change.

45
00:04:01,965 --> 00:04:04,747
So you might be saying tight, heat, et cetera.

46
00:04:05,829 --> 00:04:11,615
And make that very clear to yourself by staying there and feeling it.

47
00:04:11,995 --> 00:04:13,417
It's not an intellectual process.

48
00:04:13,437 --> 00:04:14,677
You don't want to think about it.

49
00:04:15,062 --> 00:04:27,156
It's a direct experience of these sensations, which are the information coming to us, and then the mind does something with it.

50
00:04:28,358 --> 00:04:33,343
Now, at the second level of perception, that's when the word pain comes up.

51
00:04:34,464 --> 00:04:37,507
And that's when you realize that pain is a mental construct.

52
00:04:37,908 --> 00:04:39,329
It doesn't actually exist.

53
00:04:39,511 --> 00:04:41,593
What exists are these sensations.

54
00:04:42,281 --> 00:04:45,225
Now, it's not as though you have to forget what pain is.

55
00:04:46,127 --> 00:04:47,850
You need to know when to go to the doctor.

56
00:04:48,511 --> 00:04:50,273
But there's that clarity now.

57
00:04:50,653 --> 00:05:01,588
That pain is basically a concept that we have about certain groups of sensations that arise from the body itself, right?

58
00:05:01,648 --> 00:05:03,490
So that's your sense base.

59
00:05:03,511 --> 00:05:05,473
Remember, the retina is for the eye.

60
00:05:06,334 --> 00:05:10,821
Sensations in the body are your felt, your sensation base.

61
00:05:11,964 --> 00:05:18,473
So by deconstructing something, you're taking away its substance, right?

62
00:05:19,595 --> 00:05:23,120
And by doing that, you're realizing there's actually not much there.

63
00:05:23,540 --> 00:05:33,494
And as you go into it, that sort of detail, the quality of impermanence becomes much more obvious.

64
00:05:33,514 --> 00:05:35,896
So whatever is impermanent, of course, can't be

65
00:05:36,214 --> 00:05:37,276
It can't be substantial.

66
00:05:37,317 --> 00:05:38,119
It can't be a self.

67
00:05:38,158 --> 00:05:42,447
It can't be anything because as soon as it arises, it's virtually disappeared.

68
00:05:44,271 --> 00:05:56,214
So by moving between those two perceptions, the perception of sensations, basic sensations coming in, heat, pressure, etc.,

69
00:05:56,430 --> 00:06:06,701
and the concept of pain, we're also beginning to see how the mind, how the mind builds up the world that we are living in.

70
00:06:07,281 --> 00:06:10,805
So remember, we're encased in this little bubble of consciousness.

71
00:06:10,846 --> 00:06:11,927
This is our world.

72
00:06:12,528 --> 00:06:17,692
And whenever in the scriptures the Buddha says, the end of the world, he doesn't mean the end of the cosmos.

73
00:06:17,713 --> 00:06:21,797
He means the end of the world that we constantly are creating.

74
00:06:22,620 --> 00:06:26,468
So that's one way in which we can take away substance from things.

75
00:06:27,369 --> 00:06:29,333
And you can do the same with an emotion.

76
00:06:29,934 --> 00:06:43,117
So if you're isolating now and you're stuck in a room, etc., then say sometime in the evening, especially, you might feel lonely, might feel sad, etc.

77
00:06:43,617 --> 00:06:45,961
So if that sort of negative state comes up,

78
00:06:46,194 --> 00:06:47,615
You see, you don't think about it.

79
00:06:47,857 --> 00:06:49,978
You don't start indulging this business.

80
00:06:49,999 --> 00:06:52,901
If I'm, I'm unlovable, you know, nobody loves me.

81
00:06:53,021 --> 00:06:53,682
Everybody hates me.

82
00:06:54,343 --> 00:06:54,843
Well, how is it?

83
00:06:54,862 --> 00:07:00,329
I've got no friends, but you go into the field, the field of loneliness.

84
00:07:01,149 --> 00:07:08,136
And, and as you, as you take it apart, as you begin to look at it, just as sensation, it begins to lose.

85
00:07:08,216 --> 00:07:13,802
It's that, that reality that we give it when we turn it into an emotion.

86
00:07:14,221 --> 00:07:14,903
And of course,

87
00:07:15,288 --> 00:07:19,531
it's at that point that we flip into this, I am lonely.

88
00:07:19,692 --> 00:07:22,314
So there's the identity, right?

89
00:07:22,334 --> 00:07:26,858
When it comes to the pain is I have a bad knee, there's your possession.

90
00:07:28,038 --> 00:07:38,329
So we're sort of breaking up that mental process and recognizing that the I is in a sense, the last step in the process.

91
00:07:38,968 --> 00:07:44,434
The sense of identity is the last thing that happens in that process of perception.

92
00:07:46,117 --> 00:07:49,182
Yeah.

93
00:07:49,202 --> 00:07:55,653
I mean, the one example I use, which I know some of you know, is about ice cream, you see.

94
00:07:55,994 --> 00:08:01,882
So as soon as you see ice cream, then, of course, there's the memory of it.

95
00:08:02,538 --> 00:08:07,502
And there's that sense of like, so that's the Veda, like it.

96
00:08:08,423 --> 00:08:14,629
So seeing it, it has to go through that process of recognizing it as I scream.

97
00:08:15,009 --> 00:08:16,432
And then there's the liking of it.

98
00:08:16,732 --> 00:08:18,374
Then there's the want of it.

99
00:08:18,593 --> 00:08:20,716
And then there's the I want.

100
00:08:21,156 --> 00:08:24,660
And as soon as the I comes in, it's very difficult to stop the empowerment.

101
00:08:25,240 --> 00:08:26,641
And that's the getting, right?

102
00:08:27,023 --> 00:08:32,528
So even though I say, I like, want, get, I scream, actually,

103
00:08:32,778 --> 00:08:40,644
psychologically, in Buddhist understanding, it's like, want, I, get, I scream.

104
00:08:40,684 --> 00:08:41,505
That's the process.

105
00:08:42,145 --> 00:08:47,530
And it's breaking that up that you realize that this identity is necessary, right?

106
00:08:47,551 --> 00:08:50,974
It's just something we put into that process.

107
00:08:52,735 --> 00:08:55,999
Okay, so you can always do this.

108
00:08:56,038 --> 00:08:57,720
You can start your meditation with this.

109
00:08:57,860 --> 00:09:00,523
If you find the mind's wandering, you can raise the interest.

110
00:09:00,958 --> 00:09:04,322
and go through that sort of little exercise.

111
00:09:06,807 --> 00:09:11,873
Now, the second thing is this business of powerlessness.

112
00:09:14,296 --> 00:09:17,541
We haven't got entire control of the body.

113
00:09:18,041 --> 00:09:21,586
So obviously, pain in the knee is a case in point.

114
00:09:21,725 --> 00:09:24,750
You recognize that the pain has arisen, whether you want it to or not.

115
00:09:25,110 --> 00:09:26,231
It's not under your control.

116
00:09:26,751 --> 00:09:28,394
The control center is elsewhere.

117
00:09:28,434 --> 00:09:29,495
It's in the body somewhere.

118
00:09:29,515 --> 00:09:30,256
It's in the brain.

119
00:09:30,575 --> 00:09:31,316
whatever you wish.

120
00:09:32,638 --> 00:09:40,628
And it's that disconnection with something that's happening that is disconnecting the I, right?

121
00:09:40,649 --> 00:09:42,191
So it's not all mine.

122
00:09:42,250 --> 00:09:43,131
It's not my pain.

123
00:09:43,192 --> 00:09:44,573
It's just the pain.

124
00:09:45,475 --> 00:09:50,682
And when you have something like loneliness or happiness, then it's not me and I who are happy.

125
00:09:50,961 --> 00:09:53,926
There is an emotion of happiness.

126
00:09:53,946 --> 00:09:56,529
Now, in your noting, you can make that plain to yourself.

127
00:09:56,970 --> 00:09:58,152
There is pain.

128
00:09:58,672 --> 00:10:00,014
There is happiness.

129
00:10:00,453 --> 00:10:01,635
There is sadness.

130
00:10:02,317 --> 00:10:12,451
And just by objectifying it like that, you're undermining that sense of possession and an identity.

131
00:10:14,352 --> 00:10:26,028
And when you step back into that position of the observer, the feeler, you can ask yourself, you know, can the perceiver be the perceived?

132
00:10:27,022 --> 00:10:50,058
and the perceiver be the perceived okay now that takes us on to the uh the third point which is about um um no control well that that that part is about no control it takes us to the third point which is objectify the objectivization of our experience so uh

133
00:10:50,342 --> 00:10:54,769
The two examples I use is first of all, when we were babies, we were all babies.

134
00:10:55,610 --> 00:10:57,833
And the second one is the myth of narcissus.

135
00:10:58,975 --> 00:11:12,193
So seemingly, when we're born and presumably in the later stages in the womb, for the first four months of our birth, everything we experience is just a shower.

136
00:11:12,514 --> 00:11:18,121
It's just a continual bombardment of sensations out of which we can make no sense.

137
00:11:18,557 --> 00:11:24,346
So there's light coming in, there's sounds coming in, there's sensations in the body, there's smells, there's tastes.

138
00:11:25,128 --> 00:11:26,068
And it's all one.

139
00:11:26,089 --> 00:11:27,971
It's not differentiated at all.

140
00:11:28,533 --> 00:11:29,815
That's what seems to happen.

141
00:11:30,716 --> 00:11:34,922
But of one thing, we're very sure whether something is pleasant or unpleasant.

142
00:11:34,942 --> 00:11:40,971
And when we are hungry, we definitely tell our mothers we're hungry.

143
00:11:41,203 --> 00:11:47,091
That undifferentiated catastrophe, you can say, of sensation for this poor little baby.

144
00:11:47,491 --> 00:11:50,535
No wonder it sleeps for, what, 16, 18 hours a day or something.

145
00:11:52,138 --> 00:11:56,163
Out of that, there looms, you might say, the first object.

146
00:11:56,202 --> 00:11:59,626
This is what psychologists tell you.

147
00:12:00,107 --> 00:12:03,873
And that object is usually your carer, your mother, usually.

148
00:12:04,533 --> 00:12:08,899
And very slowly over time, this differentiation occurs.

149
00:12:09,097 --> 00:12:11,860
helps to push the outer world outwards.

150
00:12:12,662 --> 00:12:23,652
And so the baby becomes to recognize that there's an outer world, although it might not be so much aware of the inner world, but it's definitely objectifying the outer world.

151
00:12:24,653 --> 00:12:32,380
And then there's also the attachment to the carer, to your mother.

152
00:12:33,182 --> 00:12:37,265
And so it seems that this business of sort of detaching

153
00:12:37,836 --> 00:12:52,270
pulling out in this too close a relationship with our parent begins to sort of break apart.

154
00:12:52,792 --> 00:12:55,173
And there's all this terrible twos and all that.

155
00:12:55,575 --> 00:12:58,857
But by the age of three, we seem to be very clear.

156
00:12:59,477 --> 00:13:01,320
I'm me and everything else is not me.

157
00:13:03,142 --> 00:13:04,243
And that's taken three years.

158
00:13:04,403 --> 00:13:06,105
That's taken three years to push

159
00:13:06,355 --> 00:13:13,365
the outer world out into an object, into something that exists outside me, as different from me.

160
00:13:14,947 --> 00:13:19,332
Now, if you think about it, that's all that's happening in our vipassana.

161
00:13:19,453 --> 00:13:27,125
We are taking a position within ourselves and pushing the inner world outwardly to observe it.

162
00:13:28,947 --> 00:13:33,813
And in so doing, we're sort of reestablishing our identities.

163
00:13:34,469 --> 00:13:43,740
So sometimes we identify with the body, especially if it's a sort of sharp pain like biting your tongue or when you're hungry or whatever.

164
00:13:45,163 --> 00:13:47,686
It's easier for us to identify with our emotions.

165
00:13:48,006 --> 00:13:48,827
I am sad.

166
00:13:49,448 --> 00:13:51,912
And then you pull yourself out of that identity.

167
00:13:52,371 --> 00:13:54,455
It's even easier to get lost in thought.

168
00:13:55,537 --> 00:13:56,717
You don't say I am thinking.

169
00:13:56,738 --> 00:13:57,840
You are thinking.

170
00:13:57,860 --> 00:13:58,941
You are the thinker.

171
00:13:59,562 --> 00:14:12,690
And you pull yourself out of that, out of the imaginative thinking, and you find yourself in this very strange position of being the inner observer, the inner feeler, the one who knows, the experiencer.

172
00:14:13,510 --> 00:14:20,085
And when we've really got that position, everything is manifesting in front of us, right?

173
00:14:20,351 --> 00:14:26,899
And that's a point where we can also recognize that all this stuff is arising whether we want it to or not.

174
00:14:27,399 --> 00:14:30,942
And that again points to this business of control.

175
00:14:31,003 --> 00:14:40,712
And something you can do when you're in that position of the observer is reflect what's it like being just the observer.

176
00:14:41,153 --> 00:14:45,398
Because in the observer, there's no emotions, there's no thoughts, and there's no sensations.

177
00:14:46,035 --> 00:14:47,496
Keep working on that, right?

178
00:14:47,596 --> 00:14:58,187
Because that identity is a fraction of the experience of Nirvana, right?

179
00:14:58,548 --> 00:14:59,989
We'll come to that another time.

180
00:15:01,409 --> 00:15:14,962
So we're into this objectivization and I want to give you another image that you can work with and that's this myth of Narcissus.

181
00:15:15,365 --> 00:15:22,654
Now, remember, Narcissus looks into the pool, this very beautiful young man, and he falls in love with himself, with the image.

182
00:15:23,496 --> 00:15:28,303
And he goes to embrace himself and, of course, falls in and drowns.

183
00:15:29,966 --> 00:15:33,331
Now, in mythology, water is creation.

184
00:15:34,011 --> 00:15:37,096
Normally, it's often used for an image for creation.

185
00:15:39,440 --> 00:15:43,245
And Narcissus is sitting on the bank.

186
00:15:43,394 --> 00:15:49,366
And he looks onto the surface of the water, into the water, and he catches his own image.

187
00:15:50,750 --> 00:15:57,465
Now, that's exactly what's happening with this satipanya, with this intuitive awareness, with the Buddha within.

188
00:15:58,246 --> 00:16:00,171
It looks into...

189
00:16:00,320 --> 00:16:06,269
The mind, the heart-mind complex, the body-heart-mind complex, the psycho-physical organism.

190
00:16:06,990 --> 00:16:12,841
And what it sees is that surface consciousness upon which everything is rising.

191
00:16:13,322 --> 00:16:14,924
And it captures its own image.

192
00:16:15,325 --> 00:16:22,277
And that's the sense of the observer, the feeler, the experiencer, the sense of presence.

193
00:16:22,730 --> 00:16:32,000
In other words, that mirror, that scheme, that surface is mirroring back to the awareness, its own presence.

194
00:16:33,082 --> 00:16:45,697
Now, what happens is, of course, you get lost, you dive into, you go into the world that the mind is creating on that very same surface.

195
00:16:46,698 --> 00:16:49,722
And that's what we're experiencing.

196
00:16:49,783 --> 00:16:51,284
That's the...

197
00:16:51,923 --> 00:17:19,887
bubble of consciousness that is our world and it's specific it's just our world i i can't know your world i've got some idea of it but i can't experience your world as your world and so everything that i experience is very personal to me it's very subjective but i'm actually creating it when i say i i mean of course the body the heart mind

198
00:17:20,509 --> 00:17:24,134
The psychophysical organism is creating the information.

199
00:17:25,096 --> 00:17:31,704
And what's happening is that this intuitive awareness is drowning in it.

200
00:17:31,965 --> 00:17:33,106
That's the delusion.

201
00:17:33,186 --> 00:17:37,272
It thinks that this is where true happiness is to be found.

202
00:17:37,333 --> 00:17:40,577
And of course, it fails.

203
00:17:42,099 --> 00:17:45,183
Our practice is to objectify all that, to see it.

204
00:17:46,045 --> 00:17:49,690
Right there in front of our inner eye, you could say,

205
00:17:49,923 --> 00:18:19,238
manifesting and in so doing of course we're pulling out this quality of intuitive awareness now that's as far as we can go by our own practice and act of will we can we can determine that right we can just push things away from us inwardly and make them an object but you can't push that sense of the observer away that just creates another self trying to get rid of the observer

206
00:18:19,386 --> 00:18:24,011
So in a sense, you can do two things.

207
00:18:24,732 --> 00:18:30,759
You can either, as it were, from that position, just glance back.

208
00:18:30,778 --> 00:18:36,945
And what I mean by glancing back is the question, what's inside this observer?

209
00:18:38,627 --> 00:18:40,289
What's inside it, right?

210
00:18:40,309 --> 00:18:43,613
We know what's inside the body, the feelings, the sensations.

211
00:18:44,074 --> 00:18:47,217
We know what's inside the heart, the emotions, the moods.

212
00:18:47,382 --> 00:18:50,185
And we know what's inside the mind, thoughts, images.

213
00:18:50,566 --> 00:18:52,449
What's inside the observer?

214
00:18:55,073 --> 00:19:01,442
And that's one way of sort of indicating that it's something which is of a completely different order.

215
00:19:01,682 --> 00:19:05,086
It doesn't belong to this psychophysical organism.

216
00:19:05,968 --> 00:19:12,376
And the next thing is that you can turn your attention towards the sense of

217
00:19:12,609 --> 00:19:26,163
self of that observer right the sense of somebody observing and just keep your eye there just wondering where is that being manufactured okay

218
00:19:27,190 --> 00:19:34,398
And after that, really, you just have to wait until the insight arises.

219
